WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:02.460
You may have heard
the expression

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:04.500
“knowledge is power.”

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.320
Well, today we’re going
to give you more power

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.180
to control your
diet and lifestyle

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.460
by giving you the facts.

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.560
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.620
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:18.900
Today we continue
our series

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.900
on how industries impact
dietary and health guidelines.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.360
And pick things up
by taking a close look

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.840
at corporate criticism of
scientific nutrition literature.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:32.160
While randomized controlled trials
are highly reliable in assessing

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.580
interventions like drugs,
they’re harder to do with diet.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:38.700
Dietary diseases can
take decades to develop.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.760
It’s not like you can
give people placebo food,

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.460
and it’s hard to get people
to stick to assigned diets,

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:47.220
especially for the years it
would take to observe effects

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.280
on hard endpoints like
heart disease or cancer.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:54.060
That’s why we have to use
observational studies

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.580
of large numbers of people
and their diets over time

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.180
to see which foods appear to
be linked to which diseases.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.020
And interestingly, if you
compare data obtained from

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:07.620
observational population studies
versus randomized trials,

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.840
on average, there is little evidence

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.420
for signiﬁcant differences
between the findings.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:15.060
Not just in the same
direction of effect,

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:18.480
but of the same general
magnitude of the effect

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.000
in about 90 percent of the
treatments they looked at.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.680
But wait, what about the hormone
replacement therapy disparity

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.420
I talked about in the last video?

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.940
It turns out when you go
back and look at the data,

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:32.100
it was just a difference
in timing in terms of

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.020
when the Premarin was started,

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.020
and they actually showed the
same results after all.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.920
But even if observational trials
did provide lower quality evidence,

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.680
maybe we don’t need the
same level of certainty

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:47.400
when we’re telling someone to eat
more broccoli or drink less soda,

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.000
compared to whether or not you want
to prescribe someone some drug.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.520
After all, prescription drugs

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.600
are the third leading cause of
death in the United States.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.340
It goes heart disease,
cancer, then doctors.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.360
100,000 Americans
are wiped out every year

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:09.540
from the side-effects of
prescription drugs taken as directed.

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:14.160
So, given the massive risks,
you better have rock-solid evidence

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.640
that there are benefits
that outweigh the risks.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.540
You are playing with fire; so,
darn right I want randomized

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.000
double-blind, placebo-
controlled trials for drugs.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.300
But when you’re just telling
people to cut down on doughnuts,

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.640
you don’t need the
same level of proof.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:33.720
In the end, the industry-funded
sugar paper concluding

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.000
that the dietary guidelines
telling people to cut down

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:38.700
aren’t trustworthy, because
they’re based on such

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.320
“low-quality evidence” is an
example of the inappropriate use

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:46.200
of the drug trial paradigm
in nutrition research.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.860
You say yeah, but what were
the authors supposed to do?

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.020
If GRADE is the way
you judge guidelines,

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:53.040
then you can’t blame them.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.840
But no, there are other tools,
like for example, NutriGrade,

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.680
a scoring system specifically
designed to assess and judge

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.840
the level of evidence
in nutrition research.

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.020
One of the things I like
about NutriGrade is that

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.600
it specifically takes
funding bias into account,

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.540
so industry-funded
trials are downgraded.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.540
No wonder the industry-
funded authors

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.780
chose the inappropriate
drug method instead.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:20.700
HEALM is another one,

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.280
Hierarchies of Evidence Applied
to Lifestyle Medicine,

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.520
specifically designed
because existing tools

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:28.440
such as GRADE are
not viable options

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.200
when it comes to questions
that you can’t fully address

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.880
through randomized controlled trials.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.500
In a way each research method
has its unique contribution.

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:42.180
In a lab, you can explore the
exact mechanisms, RCTs can prove

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.300
cause and effect, and huge
population studies can study

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:48.960
hundreds of thousands of
people at a time for decades.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.540
Take the trans fat story, for example.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:54.240
We had randomized controlled
trials showing trans fat

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.640
increased risk factors
for heart disease,

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.880
and we had population studies
showing that the more trans fats

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:01.680
people ate the more
heart disease they had.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:06.000
So, taken together, these
studies forged a strong case

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.060
for the harmful effects of trans
fat consumption on heart disease,

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:12.780
and as a consequence,
it was largely removed

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.360
from the US food supply,
preventing as many

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.760
as 200,000 heart
attacks every year.

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:21.660
Now, it’s true that we never had
randomized controlled trials

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:23.940
looking at hard endpoints,
like heart attacks and death,

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:27.660
because that would take years
of randomizing people to eat

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.460
like cannisters of Crisco every day.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:33.840
You can’t let the perfect
be the enemy of the good

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.380
when there are tens of
thousands of lives at stake.

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:40.380
Public health ofﬁcials have to work

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.780
with the best available
balance of evidence there is.

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.420
It’s like when we set
tolerable upper limits

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:47.700
for lead exposure or PCBs.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.700
It’s not like we randomized
kids to drink different levels

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.820
of lead and saw who grew up
to have tolerable brain damage.

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.160
You can’t run those
kinds of experiments;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.680
so, you have to just pull
in evidence from as many

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.320
sources as possible and
make your best approximation.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:07.440
“Even if RCTs (randomized control trials)
are unavailable or impossible to conduct,

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:10.260
there is plenty of evidence
from observational studies

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:13.080
on the nutritional
causes of many cancers,

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:16.440
such as on red meat increasing
the risk of colorectal cancer.“

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:19.740
So, if dietary guidelines
aiming at cancer prevention

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:23.040
were to be assessed with the
drug-designed GRADE approach,

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:26.100
they’d reach the same conclusion
that the sugar paper did—

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:27.300
low quality evidence.

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.620
And so, no surprise a meat-
industry-funded institution

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:34.620
hired the same dude who
helped conceive and design

00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:36.660
the sugar-industry funded study.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:41.280
And boom, lead author saying we
can ignore the dietary guidelines

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:43.500
to reduce red and processed
meat consumption,

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:47.220
because they used GRADE methods
to rate the certainty of evidence,

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:49.860
and though current dietary
guidelines recommend

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.120
limiting meat consumption,

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:53.580
their results predictably demonstrated

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.920
that the evidence was
of low quality.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:59.580
Before I dive deep
into the meat papers,

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:01.920
one last irony about
the sugar paper

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:06.900
the authors used the inconsistency
of the exact recommendations

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:10.260
across sugar guidelines over
a 20-year period to raise concerns

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:11.760
about the quality of the guidelines.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:15.660
Now obviously, we would
expect guidelines to evolve,

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:19.620
but the most recent guidelines
show remarkable consistency,

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:24.720
with one exception: the 2002
Institute of Medicine guideline

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:27.420
that said a quarter of your
diet could be straight sugar

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:29.220
without running into deficiencies.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:33.420
But that outlier was partly
funded by the Coke, Pepsi,

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:36.900
cookie, candy-funded institute
that is now saying see,

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:38.820
since recommendations
are all over the place

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:42.292
(thanks in part to us),
they can’t be trusted.

00:06:42.292 --> 00:06:45.240
In our next story, the
meat industry comes up

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:48.526
with a perversion of
evidence-based medicine.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:52.740
A series of articles published in
the Annals of Internal Medicine

00:06:52.740 --> 00:06:55.800
culminated in a recommendation
suggesting people

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:58.800
keep eating their red
and processed meat.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.680
Nutrition researchers
savaged these articles.

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:04.080
The chair of the Nutrition
at Harvard called it

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.780
“a very irresponsible public
health recommendation,”

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:11.460
and the past Harvard Nutrition
chair was even less restrained.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:16.320
“It’s the most egregious abuse of
data I’ve ever seen,” said Walter Willett

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:18.960
“There are just layers
and layers of problems.”

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:22.500
Let us start to pick
through these layers.

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:26.940
First of several serious
weaknesses was that the analyses

00:07:26.940 --> 00:07:31.080
and recommendations were largely
based the so-called GRADE criteria

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:33.300
which I talked about
in my last video.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:36.420
The authors erred in
applying the GRADE tool,

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:38.940
since that was
designed for drug trials.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:42.540
GRADE automatically scores
observational studies

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.360
as “low- or very-low” scores
for “certainty of evidence,”

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.120
which is exactly what you want

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:50.640
when you’re evaluating
evidence from drug trials.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:53.880
You want a randomized double-blind,
placebo-controlled trial

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:56.760
to prove the drug’s risks and benefits.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:00.780
However, the infeasibility for
conducting randomized clinical trials

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:03.600
on most dietary, lifestyle,
and environmental exposures

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.160
makes the criteria inappropriate in
these areas, since it would involve

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:11.520
controlling people’s daily diet
and following them for decades.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:14.760
“You can’t do a double‑blinded
placebo‑controlled trial

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.980
of red meat and other foods
on heart attacks or cancer.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:20.880
“For dietary and lifestyle
factors, it’s impossible to use

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:22.800
the same standards for drug trials.”

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:25.140
Imagine telling one group
of people to smoke

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:27.300
a pack of cigarettes every
day for the next 20 years

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:29.880
to prove that cigarettes
cause lung cancer.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:32.580
And how could you
make it double-blind,

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:35.700
have the control group
smoke placebo cigarettes?

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:39.180
Yet, in the meat papers,
they were downgrading studies

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:40.680
due to lack of blinding.

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:44.820
Well duh! In nutrition trials
how are you going to blind people

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:47.160
to the fact of what they’re eating?

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:50.280
GRADE is just the wrong
tool for diet studies.

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.080
In fact, the authors
admit that the reason

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:54.600
their recommendations
differ from all the others

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:57.720
is that other guidelines have
not used the GRADE approach.

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:01.140
And the reason is “you can’t
randomize people to smoke,

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:05.460
avoid physical exercise, breath
polluted air, or eat a lot of sugar

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:08.220
or red meat and then follow them
for 40 years to see if they die.

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:11.220
Now, that doesn’t mean
you have no evidence.

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:15.120
It just means you look at the
evidence in a more sophisticated way.”

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:18.300
And alternative approaches to
GRADE exist, like, for example,

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:22.200
NutriGrade, which have specifically
been developed to evaluate

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:25.140
evidence from studies on
nutritional and lifestyle factors

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:28.800
So, are the author’s appeals
to standards of evidence

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:31.980
motivated by a genuine interest
in getting to the bottom of it

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:35.160
or just to advance the
ﬁnancial interests of industry,

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:38.760
as the same lead author
had done previously

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:41.940
at the behest of soda
and candy companies?

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:47.460
“The tool he employed in his meat
and sugar studies could be misused

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:50.340
to discredit all sorts of well‑
established public health warnings,

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:52.860
like the link between secondhand
smoke and heart disease,

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:56.460
air pollution and health
problems, physical inactivity,

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:58.500
and chronic disease, or trans fat.”

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:02.700
Industries could use it to sow
doubt in any field where randomized

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:05.640
controlled trials are not
feasible, such as climate change.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:08.400
What are you going to
have, some placebo planet?

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:12.240
Strict adherence to GRADE
guidelines could even be used

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:14.280
to question the link between
smoking and lung cancer.

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:17.700
I know you can’t
randomize people to smoke,

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:20.580
but can’t you randomize
people to quit?

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:23.220
Here’s a randomized
controlled trial of the effect

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:26.280
on aged men of advice
to stop smoking.

00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:27.900
Of those randomized to quit,

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:31.020
13.7 percent died
within the study window,

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:34.140
whereas of those in the control group
who got no special instruction,

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:36.540
only 12.9 percent died.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:39.180
In other words, it didn’t work.

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:43.020
Disappointingly, the researchers
concluded, we find no evidence

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:45.240
at all of any reduction
in total mortality.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:49.560
Wait, so is smoking not
bad for you after all?

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:53.100
Of course not. Anyone
see the fatal flaw?

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:56.400
They didn’t randomize
people to quit.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.960
They randomized people
to advice to quit smoking.

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:03.900
It’s not like they could lock
people in a room for a few years.

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:06.420
At the last follow-up,
the stop smoking group

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:07.980
was smoking 8 cigarettes a day

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:10.440
compared to 12 cigarettes a
day in the control group;

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:13.320
so, it’s no surprise there
was no difference in mortality

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:16.020
since there was hardly
any difference in smoking.

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:19.080
And the same thing with diet.

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:22.800
There have been massive
randomized dietary trials:

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:24.060
the Women's Health Initiative,

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:26.520
the Multiple Risk Factor
Intervention Trial,

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:29.700
that wasted hundreds of
millions of dollars,

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:33.600
because people just flat out failed
to follow the dietary advice.

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:36.540
So, the groups ended up eating
similar diets at the end

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:38.280
so had similar
disease outcomes,

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:41.640
just like the randomized
smoking-quit trial.

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:45.840
It’s not like the failure was a result
of inexperienced investigators;

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:49.620
these trials were conducted by some
of the very best research teams

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:52.380
who invested enormous efforts
to achieve their goals.

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:57.060
But it just shows you can’t really
run decade-long randomized trials

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:58.860
that require changes
in eating behavior.

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:01.320
People just won’t do it.

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:04.020
Randomized controlled trials
couldn’t even show an effect

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:07.200
on mortality of smoking,
which is pretty remarkable,

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:09.480
considering that smoking is
one of the most powerful

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:11.160
known risk factors in the world.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:14.880
So basically, the foregone
conclusion putting any kind of junk

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:18.060
to the test in this manner would
echo the new meat report conclusion

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:22.140
that people should “eat whatever
they want and do whatever they want.”

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:25.020
It’s like a hijacking
of evidence-based medicine.

00:12:25.020 --> 00:12:28.200
Of course, we want the
best evidence possible,

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:31.920
but the whole process is
now being “manipulated and misused

00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:34.860
to support subverted
or perverted agendas.”

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:38.280
“It’s very exciting and
attention-grabbing to say

00:12:38.280 --> 00:12:40.260
there’s no need to
reduce meat intake.

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:44.040
It’s less exciting to say we reviewed
studies to evaluate the validity

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:48.000
using a system not meant to
evaluate the validity of these studies,

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:49.380
and what we found is nothing.”

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:53.520
When asked whether
physicians can advise persons

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:58.320
whether a salad is healthier
than a bowl full of sugar,

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:03.120
one of the senior co-authors
of the meat papers responded

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:05.580
that physicians should
tell persons that

00:13:05.580 --> 00:13:07.500
“the quality of evidence is low;

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:10.800
so, it depends almost entirely
on their preferences.”

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:16.200
“When GRADE criteria do not
allow us to strongly recommend

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:19.620
against smoking a cigarette
with your bowl of sugar,

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:24.737
we believe that alternative
grading systems are preferable.”

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:28.440
Did you know that the same
person paid by Big Sugar

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:31.140
to downplay the risks of
sugar was paid by Big Meat

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:34.810
to downplay the risks of meat?
Here’s the story.

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:37.980
A series of articles published in
the Annals of Internal Medicine

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:40.680
recommending people
just keep eating their meat

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:45.360
was decried by nutrition researchers
as irresponsible and unethical,

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:49.140
a travesty of science,
an assault on public health,

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:52.860
and the most egregious abuse of
evidence that they had ever seen.

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:57.120
There were calls for retraction
even before it was published

00:13:57.120 --> 00:14:00.660
from eminent public health leaders,
from a former US surgeon general,

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:03.480
a former president of the
American College of Cardiology,

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:07.260
and the directors of preventive
medicine and nutrition institutes

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:10.320
from Harvard, Yale,
Tufts, and Stanford.

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:14.520
In my last video I explained
the how, the method by which

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:18.600
they manipulated the science,
but never really got to the why.

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:21.480
I mean, the lead author's
similar attempt

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:23.460
to discredit the sugar guidelines

00:14:23.460 --> 00:14:27.360
was explicitly paid for by an
industry front group funded

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:33.180
by the likes of Coca-Cola, Dr. Pepper,
Mars candy bars, and Pepsi.

00:14:33.180 --> 00:14:37.140
But if you look at the meat
paper at the panelists' declared

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:40.680
conflicts of interest, they all
say they didn't have any,

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:42.480
including the lead author

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:44.040
who was involved
in the sugar study,

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:47.760
whose primary funder wasn't just
representing Big Soda and candy,

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:49.080
but the likes of McDonald's

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:52.560
and one of the largest
meat packers in the world.

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:57.060
Yet Johnston didn't disclose that
as a potential conflict of interest

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:00.780
when he switched from exonerating
sugar to exonerating meat.

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:03.180
What did he have to say for himself?

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:06.060
Even though the sugar
study was published in 2016,

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:08.160
he got the money for it in 2015,

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:12.000
outside of the three-year
mandatory disclosure window.

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:16.500
This is the same guy who said
the industry front group had no role

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:19.800
in writing their paper until
the Associated Press revealed

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:23.100
the truth, and the journal
had to publish a correction.

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:27.960
But Johnston doubled down this
time, saying "it is tenuous at best

00:15:27.960 --> 00:15:31.320
to suggest that his earlier work
on sugar had any influence

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:33.480
on how his team made the
new meat recommendations."

00:15:33.480 --> 00:15:38.160
The important thing is, "We have no
relationship with the meat industry."

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:44.400
Oh really? A few months
later the truth came out.

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:48.420
Correction in the so-called NutriRECS
panel meat recommendations.

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:53.220
Oops, Bradley Johnston failed
to indicate he had gotten a grant

00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:56.520
from Texas A&amp;M AgriLife,
which gets millions of dollars

00:15:56.520 --> 00:16:01.440
a year from the meat industry to
do things like run Beef Boot Camp,

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:04.860
or espouse the health
benefits of beef brisket,

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:08.160
or promote the celebration
of National Bacon Day.

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:12.780
After all, Texas A&amp;M AgriLife
serves pork producers

00:16:12.780 --> 00:16:15.540
to improve pork producer profitability.

00:16:16.380 --> 00:16:19.200
This is the group that not only gave

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:22.320
Dr. "no-relationship-with-
the-meat-industry" Johnston

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:25.500
a direct grant for over $75,000,

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:30.180
but they officially joined the whole
NutriRECS consortium to provide,

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:34.440
as Dr. Johnston explained,
generous support to impact

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:39.000
nutrition-related decision-making and
policy in North America and beyond.

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:42.900
Yet none of this was
disclosed in the paper.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:45.840
No even potential
conflicts of interest,

00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:48.240
yet they had formed
a partnership with an arm

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:51.480
of Texas A&amp;M partially
funded by the beef industry

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:55.140
to the tunes of millions a year
from the beef industry alone.

00:16:56.340 --> 00:17:02.040
Oops. In fact, Patrick Stover,
Mr. "no-conflicts-of-interest"

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:05.760
is the director of AgriLife.

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:08.220
And a month before the
meat paper was published,

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:11.280
Bradley Johnston was
offered and accepted

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:14.520
a tenured position
at Texas A&amp;M AgriLife,

00:17:14.520 --> 00:17:17.340
was already working for them
when it was published,

00:17:17.340 --> 00:17:21.900
but didn't think to mention it.
So when the Annals

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:23.340
initially sent out
a press release,

00:17:23.340 --> 00:17:26.340
which they later corrected,
saying no need

00:17:26.340 --> 00:17:29.580
to reduce red or processed meat
consumption for good health,

00:17:29.580 --> 00:17:32.520
they may have been simply
acting as a mouthpiece

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:34.320
for meat-industry propaganda.

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:38.280
The pseudoscience presented
in the Annals meat papers appears

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:42.000
to have been written solely
to create doubt and confusion

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:43.320
in the wider population.

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:46.620
The misleading recommendations
were not intended

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:48.900
to convince scientists,
who clearly understand

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:51.720
the nature of the relationship
between meat and health,

00:17:51.720 --> 00:17:54.000
and for that matter,
sugar and health.

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:58.860
The pseudoscience is presented
solely to create doubt and confusion

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:03.480
in the wider population.
Frankly, industry will do

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:05.820
what it needs to do to push
as much of its product

00:18:05.820 --> 00:18:09.180
into the world as it can, and so
will do what it needs to do

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:12.540
to obfuscate the relationship
between its products

00:18:12.540 --> 00:18:14.460
and human and planetary health;

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:17.880
they've done it with
tobacco, fossil fuels,

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:22.372
Monsanto's Roundup,
sugar, and now meat.

